digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=qwen3_5_397b_a17b:s41467-025-56106-3
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) The paper demonstrates a custom-designed 3D-printed photoelectrochemical device for solar-driven carbon reduction in seawater. Its boundary-layer shear flow couples BiVO4 photoanodes and Si photocathodes: the BiVO4 photoanodes acidify seawater bicarbonate to create an in situ CO2(aq) molecular flux, and the flow confines and delivers that flux to downstream Si photocathodes without CO2 bubbling. Compared with conventional diffusion-limited PEC systems, this molecular-flux PEC approach improves solar-to-fuel efficiency from 0.4% to 0.71% and increases carbon-product selectivity from 3% to 21%.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "E5" [label="(5,0,0) Dissolved carbon in seawater can be converted into carbon-based fuels or chemical products using photoelectrochemical devices powered entirely by sunlight."];
  "E15" [label="(15,0,0) In a conventional static photoelectrochemical device, diffusion is the dominant transport mechanism."];
  "E23" [label="(23,0,0) Conventional BiVO4-based photoabsorber examples have low solar-to-fuel conversion efficiency for CO2 reduction or water splitting."];
  "E38" [label="(38,0,0) A typical stepwise approach acidifies seawater to convert HCO3- to CO2 and then continuously bubbles pure CO2 into a conventional PEC device."];
  "E40" [label="(40,0,0) The continuous CO2 bubbling approach is costly and limited by low energy and carbon utilization efficiency."];
  "E48" [label="(48,0,0) The authors demonstrate molecular flux catalysis in which CO2(aq) reactants continuously flow to catalysts within a boundary layer flow."];
  "E49" [label="(49,0,0) The boundary layer flow is generated by a custom-designed and 3D-printed PEC device with tandem pairs of BiVO4 photoanodes and Si photocathodes arranged in parallel."];
  "E50" [label="(50,0,0) PEC CO2R catalysis coupled with boundary flow transport converts the low concentration of dissolved inorganic carbon in seawater, 2.3 mM HCO3-, to a flux of CO2(aq)."];
  "E51" [label="(51,0,0) The parallel device configuration can be repeated periodically for scaled-up, solar-driven, direct ocean CO2 conversion."];
  "E52" [label="(52,0,0) The boundary-layer shear flow confines a continuous flux of CO2(aq) molecules near the surface from the photoanode to the photocathode."];
  "E53" [label="(53,0,0) The BiVO4 photoanode generates H+ and acidifies HCO3- in flowing seawater under sunlight."];
  "E54" [label="(54,0,0) Flow prevents out-diffusion of CO2(aq) to bulk seawater, increases the transport flux of CO2(aq) and H+, and reduces CO2(aq) residence time for efficient downstream utilization."];
  "E56" [label="(56,0,0) The CO2(aq) flux generated in situ by the BiVO4 photoanode reaches the catalyst of the Si photocathode and is converted to gaseous CO products."];
  "E58" [label="(58,0,0) Boundary layer flow reduces the pH gradient and other concentration-limited transport losses, improving PEC device efficiency for CO2 reduction and related fuel-forming photocatalysis."];
  "E59" [label="(59,0,0) Compared with conventional static PEC devices, the flow device increases solar-to-fuel efficiency from 0.4% to 0.71%."];
  "E62" [label="(62,0,0) Boundary layer molecular flux catalysis increases the selectivity of carbon-based products such as CO from 3% to 21%, while a conventional static device requires constant CO2 bubbling for comparable selectivity."];
  "E5" -> "NROOT" [label="induction-case"];
